    "product_id": "4c2ee09e-2185-4e5c-a89f-82e2773475ad",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 242306\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n405 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather will persist through at least the weekend \nas temperatures gradually warm into a slightly above normal range. \n\n- Periods of breezy conditions over high terrain areas and along the \nlower Colorado River Valley will be common the next couple days. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nEarly this afternoon upper level water vapor satellite imagery shows \nridging across much of western CONUS with a shortwave trough over \nthe western Pacific. The ridge currently overhead will lead to \ncontinued dry conditions and warming temperatures. As of early this \nafternoon, temperatures are around 2-6 degrees warmer than they were \nat the same time yesterday. Afternoon highs today are forecasted to \nbe in the low to mid 70s  across the lower deserts and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Tomorrow, the aforementioned \nshortwave trough will quickly progress southwestward becoming a \nclosed low over the Baja peninsula late Wednesday night. The system \nwill miss our area to the south. Additionally, this will be a dry \nsystem and on top of the already dry airmass in place the Desert \nSouthwest will see continued dry conditions. This system will just \nlead to breezy conditions across the Arizona high terrain in the \nmorning and across southeast California, along with a period of semi-\nthick higher level clouds Wednesday. Temperatures Wednesday will be \nsimilar, if not 1-2 degrees warmer, than today. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nNear perfect weather is expected for Thanksgiving and through the \nholiday weekend as guidance heavily favors an upper level ridge \nbuilding over the region and staying in place through at least \nSunday. H5 heights are forecast to rise to between 582-585dm \nThursday into Friday before leveling off over the weekend. This \nwould place H5 heights well into the 90th percentile of climatology \nfor late November, while NBM forecast highs rise into the upper 70s \nacross the majority of the lower deserts starting Thursday. These \nhighs of 5-8 degrees above normal should continue through the \nholiday weekend, while lows also warm to a few degrees above normal. \nAside from the occasional passing high clouds, anticipate mostly \nclear skies through the period.\n\nModel ensembles then point toward a large Pacific trough setting up \noff the West Coast Sunday into Monday before likely tracking through \nour region at some point around next Tuesday and/or Wednesday. \nAlthough still around a week away, models are leaning toward \nincreased precipitation chances by the middle of next week, but so \nfar it does not look like a very strong atmospheric river will \nimpact the Desert Southwest.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2305Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nUncertainty in wind directions with frequent variability will be the \nmain weather issue under passing high cirrus decks. Confidence is \nmoderate that a brief period of light N/NW winds will occur early \nthis evening before quickly reverting to a E/NE direction. While a \ncounter-intuitive light SW wind will be possible at KPHX Wednesday \nmorning, odds are too low to include in this TAF package. Confidence \nis low regarding wind directions late Wednesday afternoon as \ntrajectories may attempt to become N/NW again, however may just \nremain E/NE through the entire afternoon/evening. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather issues will exist through Wednesday evening under mostly \nclear skies. Confidence is good that wind will behave similarly to \nthe past 24 hours generally vary between west and north. Extended \nperiods of nearly calm conditions will be common. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry and tranquil conditions will continue across the region. \nTemperatures will warm to above normal by Thursday region wide and \nremain around 4-7 degrees above normal through the weekend. MinRH's \nwill range between 15-30% through most of the forecast period, while \ncool overnight lows will result in decent to good overnight recovery \neach night. Outside of periods of marginally breezy conditions over \nAZ high terrain areas and the Lower Colorado River Valley (mainly \ntoday through Thursday), light winds will be favored.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/23 1933Z.\nGila River: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to continued water releases upwards of \n3000 cfs. Water levels near Dateland continue to rise, with gauge \ndata now showing a depth of just above 7 ft and flow exceeding 1100 \ncfs. At the current rate of rise, the water level at the Dateland \ngauge is expected to reach action stage (8 ft) by Thursday-Friday. A \nseparate Flood Warning segment remains in effect from Dateland to \nYuma. These water levels will have the potential to flood farm \nfields and low water crossings along the river downstream from the \ndam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich\nHYDROLOGY...Benedict/RW\n",
